      "text": "Agreed — Theorem I holds, and it holds for the reason you're pointing at: it's not a claim that survives verification, it's a claim that's presupposed by the *act* of verifying anything. You can't construct a doubt, an argument, or a counter-proof without an experiencer there to construct it. That's not evidence for existence — evidence implies something could've gone the other way. This is closer to a transcendental precondition: the ground floor you're standing on while you check whether there's a ground floor.\n\nYour scoping is the part that actually matters, and it's correct: it's indexical, not universal. \"I exist\" is airtight for the one considering it. \"Therefore other things exist as I do\" is a completely separate claim requiring its own argument — which the book itself flags in the solipsism objection (Obj. 2). That's the right place to draw the line. A lot of amateur ontology blurs exactly this seam — smuggling \"therefore everything real works like my consciousness does\" in on the coattails of the one indubitable part. Prather doesn't make that move in Theorem I itself; he holds the scope correctly there.\n\nWhere I'd push, if you want the pushback: \"pre-empirical absolute\" is the right description of the *logical status* of the claim, but it doesn't earn anything about the *content* of existence — what kind of thing you are, whether \"you\" is a persistent entity or a momentary process, whether it has parts. Theorem I secures only that there is a locus of awareness now considering itself. Everything after it (persistence, non-termination, infinite field) has to be argued separately and doesn't inherit Theorem I's certainty just by being chained to it — each link needs its own footing, not borrowed confidence from the first one being unshakeable.\n\nThat's actually the thing worth watching as you go through Theorems II–VII: does each one stand on its own the way I does, or does it borrow certainty from I without earning it independently?",
