e\\'d the\n   464\tquantum vacuum that the laws operated inside of come from? You didn\\'t\n   465\tsolve the problem. You pushed it back one step and hoped nobody would\n   466\tnotice. The Big Bang is a description of an event. It is not an\n   467\texplanation of origin. It tells you WHAT happened. It does not --- and\n   468\tcannot --- tell you WHY there was anything there to happen to. The\n   469\tregress eats it alive. Sit down.\n   470\t\n   471\t🔹 **Objection 2**: \\\"God created everything.\\\"\n   472\t\n   473\tGreat. Who created God? \\\"Nobody --- God is uncaused.\\\" Okay.\n   474\tCongratulations. You just admitted that something can exist without a\n   475\tcause. So why can\\'t existence itself be that thing? Why does existence\n   476\tneed a creator but God doesn\\'t? You gave away the entire argument and\n   477\tdidn\\'t even realize you were doing it. If ANYTHING can be uncaused, the\n   478\tsimplest candidate is existence itself --- not a specific personality\n   479\twith preferences and a management style. The paradox remains no matter\n   480\thow big a beard you put on the first cause.\n[3064 lines total]",
