      "text": "The user is misunderstanding the 3-sphere example by equating \"no edge\" with \"infinite,\" so I need to clarify that a 3-sphere is actually finite in volume despite having no boundary — it's like Earth's surface where traveling far enough loops you back to the start, not because there's nothing beyond it but because the geometry itself closes on itself. This directly contradicts their assumption that lacking an edge necessarily means infinite extent.…This is exactly the case the counterexample handles, so let me walk through it concretely instead of abstractly, because I think the confusion is genuinely in the geometry, not the logic.\n\nYou're right that \"no edge\" and \"no wall\" go together — nobody disputes that. Where the argument goes wrong is the next step: \"no edge → therefore more space beyond → therefore infinite.\" That step assumes \"no edge\" and \"unbounded extent\" are the same thing. They're not, and there's a simple physical model that shows why.\n\n**Take the surface of a sphere — a globe.** Walk in any direction, forever, and you never hit a wall, never hit an edge, never find a \"boundary\" where space stops and something else begins. In that exact sense, the surface has no edge — full agreement with the book's argument so far. But the surface also has a *finite area*. You can calculate it exactly: 4πr². It's not infinite. It's finite *and* edgeless, at the same time, with no contradiction between those two properties.\n\nHow? Because instead of stopping, the space **curves back into itself**. Keep walking \"in a straight line\" on a globe and eventually you arrive back where you started — not because you hit anything, but because the space itself loops. No wall was needed. No \"nothing on the other side\" was needed. The finitude comes from *curvature*, not from a boundary.\n\nGeneral relativity allows the same thing one dimension up: a closed 3-sphere universe — finite volume, and if you traveled far enough in a straight line you'd eventually return to your starting point, having never once encountered an edge. This isn't speculative or \"beyond current tools\" — it's a standard solution to Einstein's field equations, taught in any general relativity course.\n\nSo the book's argument — \"any edge implies more space beyond it, therefore infinite\" — only rules out *bounded* finite space. It says nothing about *curved, closed, unbounded* finite space, because that case has no edge for the regress argument to even get started on. Theorem III's proof, as written, has a gap exactly where this case would go. That's not a matter of perspective or which tools you use to measure it — it's a hole in the logical coverage of the argument itself.",
